		aliases = { "Poland–Lithuania", "First Polish Republic" },
		summary = "The **Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth**, also referred to as **Poland–Lithuania** or the **First Polish Republic**, was a [[Federation|federative]] real union between the [[Crown of the Kingdom of Poland|Kingdom of Poland]] and the [[Grand Duchy of Lithuania]], existing from 1569 to 1795. This state was among the largest, most populated countries of 16th- to 18th-century Europe. At its peak in the early 17th century, the Commonwealth spanned approximately 1,000,000 km² and supported a multi-ethnic population of around 12 million as of 1618. The official languages of the Commonwealth were [[Polish language|Polish]] and [[Latin]], with Catholicism as the state religion.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The Union of Lublin established the Commonwealth as a single entity on 1 July 1569. The two nations had previously been in a [[Personal union|personal union]] since the Krewo Agreement of 1385 (Polish–Lithuanian union) and the subsequent marriage of Queen Jadwiga of Poland to Grand Duke Jogaila of Lithuania, who was crowned as [[Władysław II Jagiełło]], [[Jure uxoris|jure uxoris]] King of Poland. Their descendant, Sigismund II Augustus, enforced the merger to strengthen frontiers of his dominion and maintain unity. As he remained childless, his death in 1572 marked the end of the Jagiellonian dynasty. It introduced an [[Elective monarchy|elective monarchy]], whereupon members of domestic noble families or external dynasties were elected to the throne for life. The Commonwealth's [[Parliamentary system|parliamentary system of government]] and [[Elective monarchy|elective monarchy]], called the Golden Liberty, were an early example of [[Constitutional monarchy|constitutional monarchy]].",
